Z||000005||StuGood||01-15-2001||05:57 PM||||Glad you brought this up.  I could use a set of steel lines - the ones in my GT (some of them, at least) are breaking up like raw macaroni.  Think it would be neat to paint them - color code them according to function, and put a matching color band on the end of the rubber hoses.<P>That way, I'd be less likely to "up mixed them get  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">"  They seem a bit pricey, but it would take some time to do your own.<P>Out of stock until February?!  Sigh - maybe it just wasn't meant tube be.    ||||reg||6
Z||000006||Tom88gt||01-15-2001||10:39 PM||||I have them on my car.  They are a great item.  I painted mine red, to match the engine.||||reg||14
Z||000007||Rodney||01-17-2001||04:10 PM||||Started on 15 sets today. They are so much work to make.  :-(<P>I'll maybe get these done in 2-3 weeks.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Tom88gt||01-20-2001||07:57 PM||||Rodney, make sure you check the lines for obstructions before you fabricate your kits.  Somebody sold you a bad steel line.  I've been trying to track down a nasty Code 32 problem for weeks.  Turns out that there is some kinda total blockage in the Throttle Body - EGR Solenoid line in your kit and absolutely no vaccum was at the far end of the line.  I ran a vaccum hose between the two points and now I can measure vaccum at the EGR and no Code 32.  Even with the line disconected at both ends, I can't even blow air through the line.||||reg||4
